\par\par \b \uc2 Aussie dlr sheds 1.5 pct on week, nears 2005 low  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Marion Rae  \par \uc2 624 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 2005\par 22:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2005 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Nov 4 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar threatened to test its 2005 low on Friday, pressured by an eroding yield advantage ahead of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy update that may entrench the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's flat interest rate outlook.  \par \par \uc2 The Aussie, down 1.5 percent this week, shifted below 74 U.S. cents overnight as the U.S. dollar surged against the euro after an inflation warning from Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan trumped less-hawkish remarks from his euro zone counterpart.  \par \par \uc2 The Aussie dollar's next big target is the 2005 low of $0.7365, struck on July 7.  \par \par \uc2 By 4 p.m. on Friday (0500 GMT) one Aussie <AUD=> fetched $0.7378/83, ending on the day's low, compared with $0.7413/18 late here on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 "We have seen good two-way interest in the Aussie around 74 U.S. cents this week as bears focused on the narrowing yield advantage battled bulls seeing stronger commodity and global growth indicators," said Greg Gibbs, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBC Capital Markets.  \par \par \uc2 "There is talk of buyers protecting barriers at $0.7375 providing interim support for the Aussie," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Market opinion is split on whether Monday's quarterly Statement on Monetary Policy (SMP) will reinstate a bias towards higher interest rates, after a run of weak data appeared to vindicate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's steady hand.  \par \par \uc2 The 5.5 percent cash rate has been unchanged since March as robust global conditions offset a cooler domestic economy.  \par \par \uc2 The RBA said in its last policy update in August that there was a 50:50 chance of either a rate hike or a rate cut. And recent data showed inflation remained within the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2-to-3 percent target band in the third-quarter.  \par \par \uc2 "A week or two ago the way ahead seemed pretty clear. The domestic economy showed signs of stirring ... The consensus now seems to be that any signs of toughness in next week's SMP should be seen as no more than jawboning," said Michael Blythe, chief economist at Commonwealth Australia Bank.  \par \par \uc2 "Implicitly, the current neutral bias will remain in place," he said.  \par \par \uc2 On the charts, the Aussie has retraced 50 percent of its rally from a 2004 trough of $0.6775 to a 2005 peak of $0.7990 in March. The next Fibonnaci hurdle, the 61.8 percent retracement of that rally, cuts in around $0.7240.  \par \par \uc2 "Support will be solid at $0.7360/80, roughly the calendar year low, but a break below here would be very negative," said John Kyriakopoulos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at National Australia Bank.  \par \par \uc2 Overnight, ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet left the key euro-zone interest rate at 2 percent, as expected, but gave no strong signal of a rate rise by the end of the year, trampling on speculation about a December rise.  \par \par \uc2 That saw selling on the crosses abate, easing some pressure after the Aussie hit multi-month lows this week against the euro <AUDEUR=R>, Swiss franc <AUDCHF=R> and sterling <AUDGBP=R>.  \par \par \uc2 The Aussie firmed against a broadly weaker yen <AUDJPY=R> on Friday. The U.S. dollar hit a two-year high against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  <JPY=> and euro/yen <EURJPY=> corrected from its slide of about one yen a day earlier.  \par \par \uc2 The Aussie has also climbed off this week's five-month low against the kiwi dollar <NZD=>, lifting the cross rate <AUDNZD=R> back above NZ$1.0725, partly on speculation the Reserve Bank of New Zealand might soon \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the kiwi dollar.  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 AUSTRALIA\b0 \highlight\uc2 -DOLLAR (UPDATE 2)|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|AUF|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020051103e1b3001zu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
