\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Australian Bonds Down Late Ahead Of US Payrolls  \b0\par\par\uc2 536 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Dezember 2004\par 08:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY (Dow Jones)--The Australian dollar was lower against the U.S. dollar late Friday as investors migrated to the sidelines ahead of U.S. payrolls data and all its inherent uncertainties, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 Government bond prices also closed lower, with the December three-year bonds settling at 95.00, a critically important juncture for the market.  \par \par \uc2 The strength or weakness of the payrolls number is set to drive bond trading next week.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers warned that if U.S. employment growth outstrips expectations, the strongly priced local bond market could fall sharply in coming sessions.  \par \par \uc2 According to a Dow Jones Newswires survey, the median expectation for payrolls is a gain of 200,000, following October's 337,000 rise.  \par \par \uc2 Around 0630 GMT, the Australian dollar was at US$0.7744, down from US$0.7808 late Wednesday in Sydney. Against the yen, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at Y80.08, compared with Y79.76.  \par \par \uc2 Aside from the lack of predictability surrounding monthly U.S. payrolls data, strategist at the National \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Bank John Kyriakopoulos said that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains a wildcard.  \par \par \uc2 While coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the U.S. dollar appears highly unlikely, any move by a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at this point has the potential to fan volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, he said.  \par \par \uc2 In the case of the Australian dollar, Kyriakopoulos said that solid support should emerge if it returns to its week's lows around US$0.7700 later Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Medium term, the major issue for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is shifting ideas on the outlook for official interest rates, he said.  \par \par \uc2 NAB changed its official view Friday that interest rates would rise again. It now sees no change in the 5.25% cash rate all the way through 2005.  \par \par \uc2 Confirmation of a more dovish position on interest rates at the Reserve Bank could come late this month when Deputy Reserve Bank Governor Glenn Stevens makes a speech on current economic conditions.  \par \par \uc2 Any comment by Stevens on the rise of the Australian dollar and its deflationary consequences would confirm to many that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will leave interest rates on hold over the long term.  \par \par \uc2 It said earlier this month that while the Reserve Bank saw no pressing need to raise interest rates, it was of the view that they would need to rise again one more time.  \par \par \uc2 NAB's Kyriakopoulos said that if anything, Stevens would want to first damp expectations of a rate cut, which is currently priced into the debt markets.  \par \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires survey of economists this week was still showing a majority expecting interest rates to rise again.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    10.00% Oct. 15, '07:     4.96%  vs. 4.93% \par    6.25% Apr. 15,  '15:     5.30%  vs. 5.23% \par    3-10 Yr Yield Spread:    0.34   vs. 0.30 \par    10-Yr Spread To U.S.:    0.90   vs. 0.88 \par    SFE Sep 3Y Futures:      95.00  dn  0.02 \par    SFE Sep 10Y Futures:     94.710 dn  0.040 \par    3-10 Yr Futures Spread:  0.29   vs. 0.27 \par    (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  prices supplied by Reuters.) \par  \par -By James Glynn; Dow Jones Newswires; 612-8235-2955; james.glynn@dowjones.com \par    -Edited by Melanie Botts \par   \f28 \par \pa