\par\par \b \uc2  Business in Asia Today -- Sept. 26, 2002 \b0\par\par\uc2 1,291 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2002\par 13:28\par \uc2  PR Newswire \par \uc2 PRN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2002, PR Newswire) \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY, Australia, Sept. 26 /PRNewswire/ -- A summary prepared by Asia Pulse ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.asiapulse.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.asiapulse.com}}}\uc2 ), the real-time, Asia-based wire with exclusive news, market intelligence and business opportunities:     AUSTRALIA'S COLES MYER EXPECTED TO ACHIEVE FORECAST FY PROFIT    MELBOURNE - Ailing retailer Coles Myer Ltd (ASX:CML), rocked by damaging boardroom squabbles and ongoing uncertainty about its Myer Grace Bros structure, will hit its forecast full year net profit of A$350 million (US$189.88 million) next week - but only just. Analysts believe a significant item or two, most likely in the interest and tax area, will get Coles Myer over the line. After downgrading its initial forecast for net profit for 2001/02 of A$400 million (US$217 million), Coles Myer warned the market in August that it was likely to achieve the lower end of its revised A$350 million (US$189.88 million)-A$365 million (US$198.01 million) expectation. \par \par \uc2       JAPAN'S LEOPALACE21 TO RECORD 47 PCT GAIN IN GROUP OPERATING PROFIT    TOKYO - Leopalace21 Corp. (TSE:8848) is expected to record a consolidated operating profit of 13.5 billion yen (US$110.1 million) for the fiscal half through September 30, a year-on-year increase of 47 per cent. The amount would be 1 billion yen above the forecast to date and a record-high interim result. Group sales are predicted to reach 154 billion yen, a year-on-year gain of 21 per cent and a new record high.      KOREA'S HYNIX TO SELL TFT-LCD UNIT TO CHINA'S BOE TECHNOLOGY    SEOUL - Hynix Semiconductor Inc. signed a memorandum of understanding today with Boe Technology Group [BOE] to sell its thin film transistor-liquid crystal display unit, Hydis, the Korean chipmaker said. Under the agreement, the world's third largest chipmaker will sell assets and buildings related to the TFT-LCD business to the Chinese company for US$380 million. This sum though might be changed based on an asset evaluation. The two companies plan to complete the sales negotiations by the end of November with the formal accord likely to be sealed by Oct. 25, Hynix said.     JAPAN CONSUMER ELEC APPLIANCE SALES POST FIRST GAIN IN 17 MONTHS    TOKYO - Sales of consumer electronics and appliances at the 2,909 stores affiliated with the Nippon Electric Big-Stores Association [NEBA] in August showed their first year-on-year increase in 17 months, gaining 1.1 per cent to reach 204.4 billion yen (US$1.67 billion). Seasonal and big-ticket products such as refrigerators and air conditioners led the way, encouraged by particularly hot weather in late July and early August. Air conditioner sales increased 4.7 per cent from a year earlier, while refrigerator sales grew 6.7 per cent. New products such as odor-free, energy-saving refrigerators also helped boost the upward trend.     KOREA'S \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  EQUALIZATION FUND TO POST US$1.1 BLN DEFICIT    SEOUL - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Equalization Fund is likely to post over 1.5 trillion won (US$1.1 billion) in losses from \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the end of the year, an official of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy [MOFE] said today. The figure marks a sharp rise from 660 billion won a year earlier. Ministry officials attribute the rising deficit to government plans to issue five trillion won of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  equalization bonds by the end of the year. This is up from last year's 3.6 trillion won and is to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in drastic fluctuations in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.     \b \highlight22\uc2 AUSTRALIA\b0 \highlight\uc2 'S BRUMBY'S BAKERIES BOOKS US$1 MLN PRETAX PROFIT, MULLS LISTING    BRISBANE - Bakery franchise chain Brumby's Bakeries Holdings Ltd today booked a record result for 2001/02, and flagged a possible listing on the Australian Stock Exchange [ASX]. Brumby's was aiming to expand its franchise network by at least 10 per cent over the next 12 months and was looking to push into Asian markets. The 2002 fiscal year was the most successful in Brumby's 26-year history, with total sales peaking at A$135 million (US$73.24 million), from A$120 million (US$65.1 million) previously. Brumby's reported a 20 per cent increase in pre-tax profit to A$1.85 million (US$1.0 million).     SAMSUNG TEAMS UP WITH MITSUBISHI IN MICROCHIP DEVELOPMENT    SEOUL - Samsung Electronics Co. (KSE:05930) and Mitsubishi Electronic Corp. have formed a strategic alliance to enhance cooperation in the core chip solutions sector for mobile handsets equipped with a micro video camera. Under the arrangement, Samsung and Mitsubishi plan to develop technologies for next-generation semiconductors that are compatible with each other by the end of the year. Samsung will offer the Japanese firm its technology for complementary metal oxide semiconductors [CMOS] image sensor [CIS], while Mitsubishi will make its image signal procession [ISP] technology available to the Korean firm. HYUNDAI HEAVY, DOOSAN GROUP SUFFER MAJOR LOSSES IN STOCK VALUE    SEOUL - Hyundai Heavy Industries (KSE:09540) and Doosan Groups (KSE:00150) saw their market capitalization plunge the most among the 20 largest affiliates of chaebols listed on the stock exchange, the Korea Stock Exchange said today. The stock exchange said the market capitalization of Hyundai Heavy Industries Group fell 49 per cent from 2.68 trillion won (US$2.2 billion) to 1.33 trillion won during the period April 18 until September 25. Doosan Group dropped 48 per cent to 973.3 billion won from two trillion won during the same period.     INDONESIA'S IBRA TO SELL REMAINING ASSET IN INDOSIAR    JAKARTA - The Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency [IBRA] is expected to sell its remaining 8.26 per cent stake in PT Indosiar Visual Mandiri (JSX:IDSR) in October, a report said today. PT Indosiar Visual Mandiri is a listed operator of one of the country's private television broadcasting stations and is one of more than 100 subsidiaries of the Salim Group pledged to IBRA as compensation for the conglomerate's debt to the government. IBRA already sold a 49 per cent stake in PT Indosiar at a price of Rp775 billion (US$86 million) to TDM Asset Management. An IBRA executive Taufik Mappaenre Ma'roef said IBRA is looking for an underwriter to handle the divestment plan.     HYUNDAI MERCHANT MARINE TO REDEEM OUTSTANDING US$253 MLN TO KDB    SEOUL - Hyundai Merchant Marine [HMM] will pay back outstanding loans of 310 billion won (US$253 million) in total to the Korea Development Bank [KDB] next month, company officials said today. In June 2000, KDB extended a total of 490 billion won to the Hyundai affiliate - 400 billion won in overdraft facilities and 90 billion won in loans. HMM has repaid 170 billion won and 10 billion won respectively. HMM is planning to repay the outstanding loans when it receives proceeds from the sale of auto-shipping vessels worth 1.8 trillion won (US$1.47 billion) at the end of next month.     (C) Asia Pulse Pte Ltd. Each day Asia Pulse creates up to 300 items of news, business opportunities, expert commentary and industry profiles. Asia Pulse is a unique joint venture involving the resources of Asia's major news and information groups:    (AAP) - AAP Information Services Pty Ltd (Australia)    (ANTARA) - LKBN ANTARA (Indonesia)    (CNA) - Central News Agency (Taiwan)    (IRNA)- Islamic Republic News Agency (Iran) (Nikkei) - Nihon Keizai Shimbun Inc (Japan)    (ONA) - Oman News Agency (Oman) (PNA) - Philippines Ne