\par\par \b \uc2  Aussie looks weary with nothing new on WMC. \b0\par\par\uc2 497 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2001\par 08:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SYDNEY, Oct 18 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar looked tired at just below 51 U.S. cents on Thursday as stale long positions were cut with nothing new to peg an extension of this week's rally on, and with no news of a bid for miner WMC Ltd . \par \par \uc2  The Aussie rallied some two percent at the start of the week, touching a one-month high of 51.73 cents on speculation that the miner was a target, and while WMC confirmed on Wednesday it was in talks with Alcoa and others, a deal stayed under wraps. \par \par \uc2  "If there is an actual cash bid for Western Mining, there's going to be a bottleneck and you will see the Aussie higher. There is something in the air...but if it's a scrip you will see the Aussie sink," said Greg McKenna, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at National Australia Bank. \par \par \uc2  "But we all know this is an event that is in complete contradiction to the trend and the global backdrop," McKenna said, who expects the Aussie to range trade 50.50-51.50 cents in the next day or so. \par \par \uc2  At 4:23 p.m. (0623 GMT) the AUD was $0.5084/94 compared with $0.5144/49 late here on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  M&A activity aside, there is little positive for the Aussie to look at with the global economy weak and continued anthrax scares in the United States and elsewhere likely to knock already fragile consumer and market confidence. \par \par \uc2  The Aussie's pullback has left the 51.70/70 cent area as critical resistance, being the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 200-day moving average, and also a 61.8 percent retracement of the sell-off from 53.94 cents in August to September's double bottom of 48.20 cents. \par \par \uc2  HEAVY \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  IN SEPTEMBER \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monthly bulletin showed it had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  quite heavily in September as it attempted to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the backlash of the U.S. attacks on September 11 as investors turned risk averse. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed it bought a net A$778 million of Aussie dollars on the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, its third consecutive month of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the eleventh month of purchasing in 13 months. \par \par \uc2  It was the largest amount in one month since November last year when it bought A$860 million. \par \par \uc2  The Australian Treasury department went through the motions of releasing updated budget projections as part of the "Charter of Budget Honesty" ahead of the November 10 election. \par \par \uc2  However, the report was completely foreshadowed by Treasurer Peter Costello's mid-year budget review, released on Wednesday and months earlier than usual, and said nothing different. \par \par \uc2  The reports showed growth in 2001/02 is expected to be 3.0 percent rather than 3.25 percent as per the May budget, and the budget surplus to be just A$500 million instead of A$1.5 billion. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011018dxai02ifz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
