\par\par \b \uc2  Vulnerable Aussie dlr has fragile grip on 49c. \b0\par\par\uc2 541 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2001\par 09:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SYDNEY, Sept 21 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar had a fragile grip on 49 U.S. cents going into European trading, pulling back from failed attempts towards 49.5 cents as the USD perked up from more Bank of Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen. \par \par \uc2  The Aussie has suffered a dire week, dropping nearly three cents, and five in the past month, as investors ditch non-core \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets in an uncertain global environment. This has been just as devastating on the major crosses. \par \par \uc2  "When sentiment swings against a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the impact can be dramatic," said Craig James, chief economist at Commonwealth Securities. \par \par \uc2  The Aussie's trade weighted indexed matched Wednesday's record low of 46.6 at Friday's close. \par \par \uc2  At 4:23 p.m. (0623 GMT) the AUD was $0.4900/05 compared with $0.4930/35 late here on Thursday and close to this week's five-month low of $0.4894. \par \par \uc2  "World investment opinion has turned against commodity exporters. It seems only a matter of time before the record low...is replaced with an even lower figure," James said. \par \par \uc2  James said the unit is being hit by the risk of global recession, which would mean lower prices and demand for commodities, and the impact of the U.S. attacks on in-bound tourism, both resulting in less capital inflow into Australia. \par \par \uc2  The Aussie's leg-down this week has seen it well entrenched back below its 21-month declining trendline and leaves it at risk of retesting its April 2 47.75 cents all time low, although it is extremely oversold on charts. \par \par \uc2  BUSH STANDS FIRM \par \par \uc2  Investors have also shunned peripheral \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a rush to safe havens after the hijacked plane attacks on New York and Washington on September 11. \par \par \uc2  Share markets have also been pummelled in the past week, led by more than a 1,200-point loss on Wall Street, as investors fret about a U.S. recession, but U.S. markets could be lifted by a rousing speech by President Bush on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Bush demanded Afghanistan's ruling Taliban turn over to U.S. authorities all leaders of Osama bin Laden's militant group and close their training camps. \par \par \uc2  "These demands are not open to negotiation or discussion," Bush said in a speech to a joint session of Congress. \par \par \uc2  The local share market finished 1.39 percent down after opening over 2.5 percent down. \par \par \uc2  The Aussie has received some sort of prop from rumoured Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past week, although this has been more of a smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  rather than trying to buck the negative trend. \par \par \uc2  However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been deflated by the RBA's absence in a global rate cutting by the world's major central banks led by the Federal Reserve in an attempt to shore up confidence and soothe potentially volatile markets. \par \par \uc2  RBA governor Ian Macfarlane indicated this week that he wanted to wait until the monthly meeting on October 2 before making any alterations to policy. \par \par \uc2  A Reuters poll of economists on Friday found a large majority expecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to come to the party with a quarter point easing the day after that meeting. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010921dx9l00at0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
