\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Australia's  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  supported A$, worst over. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Victoria Thieberger \par \uc2 426 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Dezember 2000\par 07:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SYDNEY, Dec 21 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the local dollar to the tune of A$860 million in November as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plumbed record lows, the Reserve Bank said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  But analysts believe further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has totalled A$1.466 billion in the past three months, may not be needed as the Australian dollar appears to have turned the corner. \par \par \uc2  The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has recovered as the U.S. dollar has weakened, trading on Thursday at US$0.5520, more than four cents cents above its record low of $0.5070 set on November 22. \par \par \uc2  At its lows, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had fallen by more than 20 percent for the year. \par \par \uc2  In its monthly bulletin, the Reserve Bank of Australia said it bought a net A$860 million Australian dollars in the spot \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market during November. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said they thought the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had spent more during November, given the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dramatic moves on several trading days. \par \par \uc2  At the height of the Asian crisis, the RBA spent a net A$3.87 billion to try to support the Aussie from May to September 1998. \par \par \uc2  BANG FOR RBA'S BUCK \par \par \uc2  "They have obviously been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  only when they felt it necessary, and they have been quite effective given the amount spent," said NAB market strategist Greg McKenna. \par \par \uc2  "Given what has happened in the past week on the U.S. dollar, you have to say the (U.S.) dollar hegemony has ended," he said. \par \par \uc2  Growing concerns about a slowdown in the U.S. economy have weighed on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stocks over the past week. \par \par \uc2  Thursday's published figure is not necessarily the total \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the RBA, as it is only a net spot figure. The RBA can also use options to support the Australian dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank said its outstanding forward \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  commitments rose to A$25.113 billion in November from A$21.336 billion in October. \par \par \uc2  The current period of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the first reported by the RBA since September 1998, when it spent a net A$355 million. \par \par \uc2  "The A$860 million is less than what the market would have been looking for. But I don't think the markets are as interested now as they were back at 51 U.S. cents," said Macquarie Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Joanne Masters. \par \par \uc2  "The general consensus is that you've seen the historic low in the Aussie," she said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dwcl09ay7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
