\par\par \b \uc2  Asian gold opens up and firms ahead RBA report. \b0\par\par\uc2 412 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2000\par 04:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Nov 16 (Reuters) - Asian gold opened higher on Thursday morning, bolstered by a relatively flat Aussie and despite weak regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the euro's struggle to hold post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels. \par \par \uc2  Gold was expected to continue to trade in a narrow range, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Spot gold opened at US$264.80/5.30 an ounce, up from New York's last quoted US$264.55/5.05 an ounce overnight and London's late afternoon fix of US$264.60. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar was holding just above US$0.52 at 0230 GMT, supported by concerns of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  activity. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBA), which was rumoured to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the Aussie on Wednesday, will issue a bulletin at 0400 GMT and could detail its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market in October and more recently, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Gold could get a minor boost from that report, but most dealers were not expecting any major move at this time. \par \par \uc2  "Many things are working against (a rise) in the gold price, not least of which is a strong U.S. dollar and weak domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially in producer countries and the major consumer countries," said John Liau, vice president at Overseas Union Bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "I think that we are going to end the day pretty much where we started," Liau said. \par \par \uc2  Gold has been trading in a tight range of about US$2.50 or less for the past two weeks, he added. \par \par \uc2  The euro was also trapped in a narrow range of US$0.8573-US$0.8583 and below the chart critical US$0.86 level. \par \par \uc2  Demand for physical gold remains lacklustre this year, despite the current Asian holiday season when demand normally picks up. Sydney-based traders have noted a small pick-up in orders but cautioned that volumes remain light. \par \par \uc2  Tael gold, which is traded on the Chinese Gold and Silver Exchange, opened at HK$2,469 per tael, down from Wednesday's close at HK$2,471. \par \par \uc2  Spot silver opened at US$4.66/4.69 in line with Wednesday's trade. Spot silver fixed at a new three-year low of US$4.66 an ounce in London on Wednesday and ended at US$4.66/4.68 in New York. \par \par \uc2  Very little silver is traded during the Asian day, dealers said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Subscribers can click on\tab \tab   and\tab \tab   to\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par view prices in the region.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwbg01so6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
