\par\par \b \uc2  Aussie dollar erodes in Europe, eyes record lows. \b0\par\par\uc2 509 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2000\par 13:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Nov 15 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar retreated to within sight of record lows against the U.S. dollar in Europe on Wednesday, giving back gains it had made earlier on talk of Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its support. \par \par \uc2  Although the Aussie dollar had spiked up about a half cent in Sydney trading on rumoured RBA buying, traders said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone could not remove the broad-based downward pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Whilst \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be able to buy a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  some short-term stability, it can only turn a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around if fundamentals are supporting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the same time," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Barclays Capital. "That (support) is very debatable in Australia because a lot of ex-oil commodity prices are very weak - Australia's exports are still dominated by commodities - and there is the prospect that global growth would turn down next year." \par \par \uc2  Traders noted that higher Aussie dollar levels after spike-ups on RBA \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or rumours of such \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action had tended to be seen as an opportunity to sell the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "Everyone is bearish on the Aussie but they're reluctant to sell it at the lows. So there's a sell-on-rally mentality ingrained in the market at the moment," said a dealer at a Japanese bank in London. \par \par \uc2  He added, however, that losses have been limited by wariness that the RBA could step in again. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the RBA confirmed in its semi-annual statement that it had been in the market several times in recent weeks to reinforce what it said was an emerging trend towards stability in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Aussie dollar shed nearly half a cent from the day's peaks around US$0.5240 , to relapse to the $0.52-area by European midsession - within a cent of last month's record lows around US$0.5110. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said a A$4.3 billion (US$2.2 billion) budget surplus for the fiscal year ending next June forecast by the Australian government on Wednesday, up 54 percent from a A$2.8 billion projected in the May budget, had so far had little impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. The New Zealand dollar also drifted off its early highs, tracking the Australian dollar. The kiwi dollar was last trading at around US$0.3970 , about a quarter cent off five-day peaks hit earlier but still a touch above late Tuesday levels. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar was little changed around C$1.5470 per U.S. dollar , pinned within sight of last week's 22-month lows around C$1.55. \par \par \uc2  Foley said the Canadian dollar could find some lift if U.S. technology stocks maintain Tuesday's recovery gains, given a high concentration of technology shares in the TSE 300 , Canada's main stock index. \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, the TSE 300 gained nearly 1.7 percent to snap a six-day losing streak as the U.S. Nasdaq composite rose 5.78 percent. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwbf01zdp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
