\par\par \b \uc2  Australia's RBA says bought A$ several times. \b0\par\par\uc2 244 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2000\par 01:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SYDNEY, Nov 13 (Reuters) - The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  confirmed on Monday it had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  several times in recent weeks to support the ailing Australian dollar, which it said had started to stabilise. \par \par \uc2  "In recent weeks, the Bank has entered the market on several occasions to support the exchange rate and reinforce the emerging tendency towards stability," it said in its semi-annual statement on monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  The Aussie dollar has slumped by more than 20 percent over the year against the U.S. dollar, and also fallen heavily on a trade-weighted basis. Two weeks ago it struck a fresh record low of 51.1 U.S. cents. \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank devoted five pages to chronicling this year's decline of the dollar, including a chronology of large moves which went against most expectations and solid economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was last rumoured to be in the market buying dollars on November 2, when the dollar spiked one and a half cents to 53.4 U.S. cents in less than an hour, and on the following day. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has fallen from a high of 60.88 U.S. cents in January to its low of 51.1 cents on October 31. \par \par \uc2  On a trade weighted basis, the dollar was at a seven year low of 48.2 on 31 October. It was quoted just above that at 48.4 on Monday morning. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwbd02a2t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
