\par\par \b \uc2  Australia RBA: Won't Hike Rates To Protect A$ \b0\par\par\uc2  By Clyde Russell \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 722 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2000\par 09:35\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MELBOURNE -(Dow Jones)- Reserve Bank of Australia Governor Ian Macfarlane said Thursday that the embattled local dollar will recover and that monetary policy won't be used to help it. \par \par \uc2  In a speech defending the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  head said markets had overshot the level of the Australian dollar and some of the explanations for its weakness didn't stand up to scrutiny. \par \par \uc2  His main reason for the poor performance of the Australian dollar this year is the strength of the U.S. dollar, which has also pushed the Euro to record lows. \par \par \uc2  Macfarlane ruled out using monetary policy to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although he said the exchange rate is a factor in deciding the level of interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Economic conditions are still supporting further growth, he said, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains committed to ensuring that inflation stays within the 2-3% target band. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar has lost more than 20% of its value against the U.S. dollar this year, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice in recent months to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against speculators. \par \par \uc2  Macfarlane said the local dollar has experienced four major falls above 10% since it was floated in 1983. \par \par \uc2  "However, on each occasion it recovered and it will do so again this time," he told a dinner function of the Committee for the Economic Development of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in late September and last week, appeared aimed at smoothing an illiquid and volatile market rather than reversing negative sentiment which has seen the unit make an all-time low of US$0.5135, only one cent lower than Sydney's Thursday close. \par \par \uc2  The governor also gave some insight into why the Reserve Bank didn't raise rates after its board meeting this week and at two previous meetings. \par \par \uc2  "As can be seen from our current behavior, we have no intention of departing from our medium-term approach in an ad hoc attempt to push up the exchange rate for its own sake," he said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will look through the once-off factors contributing to inflation, such as fuel prices and the 10% goods and services tax, but the exchange rate will be taken into account, Macfarlane said. \par \par \uc2  "We still have to ensure that the medium-term outlook for inflation stays on track, which means making sure that several temporary factors have only a once-off effect and aren't reflected in the ongoing inflation rate," he said. \par \par \uc2  Macfarlane said the period ahead will be uncertain, but Australia has coped with this before. \par \par \uc2  "Over the past year, we have had the unusual combination of strong domestic growth, a falling current account deficit and a declining exchange rate, all of which are conducive to future growth." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Rejects Market Views On Australian Dollar Fall\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The governor devoted the bulk of his speech to his views on why the\par Australian dollar had fallen over the year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The principal reason for the fall over recent months has been the\par rising U.S. dollar, which has affected us directly and indirectly," he\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Another contributing factor was the expectation earlier in the year\par that Australian interest rates wouldn't rise as high as those in the\par U.S. "plus the intrinsic tendency for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to\par continue to go in one direction, to overshoot, and to come up with\par explanations to justify the overshooting".\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The last factor is that in recent months "there has been a tendency\par for markets to judge countries and their exchange rages by the 'new\par model economy'," Macfarlane said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Ironically, this is exactly the period when U.S. technology stocks\par have languished."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par While the argument that the Australian dollar is weak due to the large\par current account deficit isn't convincing, it is clear that investors\par appear not to be investing in\par Australia and this has hurt the Australian dollar.   But he said this\par situation appeared to be temporary and "will be seen as a phase\par through which capital markets passed", pointing to Australia's high\par use of technology, economic performance and its ability to match or\par surpass the U.S. in most economic measures.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Reserve Bank website:\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par www/rba.gov.au\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Clyde Russell, Dow Jones Newswires, 61 403 219419\par clyde.russell@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dwb905g54\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
