\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP:  \highlight22\uc2 Australia \highlight\uc2  Dollar Nervous Amid New RBA  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 458 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 2000\par 07:07\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY -(Dow Jones)- The Australian dollar is trading warily late in Sydney Friday, having been hauled higher during the day by new talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank, which has left dealers reluctant to sell the unit short. \par \par \uc2  The seesaw ride of the Australian dollar continued after Thursday's dramatic buying by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sent the unit up a cent and half, before selling in offshore trade drove it back down again. \par \par \uc2  Renewed talk of the Reserve Bank bidding helped the Australian dollar off its Friday morning lows, before the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilized around US$0.5235. \par \par \uc2  At 0550 GMT, the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at US$0.5236, down from US$0.5291 late Thursday in Sydney. Against the yen the local unit was quoted at Y56.63, down from Y57.22 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "There is quite a bit of uncertainty out there," said a senior dealer at an Australian bank. "Nobody wants to get too short with the Reserve Bank around." \par \par \uc2  The bank's moves Friday weren't reported by dealers to be as extensive as Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which caught the market offguard. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Australian dollar's fortunes in offshore trade are likely to be linked to what happens with the euro and whether U.S. October payroll data adds to the view of a slowing U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  But the unit will have to get past US$0.5280 resistance to convince that it has turned and is ready to rally. \par \par \uc2  The focus is also turning to the Reserve Bank board meeting Tuesday, with any monetary policy announcement expected Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Although the majority of analysts don't expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to lift rates, they now see an increasing risk of a move given the Reserve Bank's apparent discomfort with the weak Australian dollar and its threat to inflation. \par \par \uc2  The chances of a rate rise were reflected more clearly in the bond market, where short-end yields rose and bank bill futures also started to price in the chance of a rate rise. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "You can see it in the bill strip," said Su-Lin Ong, senior economist\par at RBC Dominion, but she added the market is still seeing the chance of\par a rate hike as less than 50%.\par    9.50% Aug. bond '03:   6.02%  vs.  6.01%\par    7.50% Sep. bond '09:   6.13%  vs.  6.13%\par    SFE Dec 3Y Futures:   93.98   unchanged\par    SFE Dec 10Y Futures:  93.855  up   0.010\par    3-10 Yr Yield Spread   0.11   vs.  0.12\par    10-Yr Spread To U.S.:  0.40   vs.  0.41\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -by Clyde Russell; Dow Jones Newswires; 612-8235-2958;\par clyde.russell@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1054 22765 4109 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dwb305ari\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
