\par\par \b \uc2  What To Do With The Aussie Now? \b0\par\par\uc2  By Nicholas Hastings \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 691 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2000\par 16:58\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  LONDON -(Dow Jones)- The Aussie dollar is starting to look like a serious basket case. \par \par \uc2  It has failed to take advantage of the latest investor disappointment in the dollar. \par \par \uc2  It has failed to bounce in line with the euro. \par \par \uc2  It has failed to benefit from good economic fundamentals. \par \par \uc2  And now, it is failing to get any real help from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  waded into the market early Thursday in what seems to have been a last desperate attempt to kick start the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the week it had dived to a new record low at U.S.$0.5112 after starting the year closer to U.S.$0.6700. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was said to have been aggressive and prolonged, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  still being seen in action at the start of the European day. \par \par \uc2  "Given the recent rally in euro/dollar and the 'apparent' market linkages between the two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Australian dollar's performance has been extremely disappointing most recently, hence the RBA move," said Mitul Kotecha, global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Lyonnais Indosuez. \par \par \uc2  Initially, the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  certainly had the desired effect, sending the Aussie back up to U.S.$0.5335. \par \par \uc2  However, as the RBA retired, so did all other support and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was soon on the slide again, being quoted late in the European day well back under U.S.$0.5200. \par \par \uc2  The Aussie's weakness came as a surprise for many, who had assumed that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has suffering largely from being lumped with the euro - in other words, investors were simply ignoring it while the U.S. was providing a more attractive investment opportunity. \par \par \uc2  Over the last week or so, though, there has been a distinct if only temporary shift in investor sentiment. Strong indications that the U.S. economy is slowing more than expected have boosted the argument that U.S. assets are becoming less attractive. \par \par \uc2  A main beneficiary of this shift has been the euro - which for the first time in many months is actually finding fairly good buying support as it struggles up from its record lows. \par \par \uc2  But, the Aussie has most definitely not joined the party. \par \par \uc2  Despite favorable consumer price data last week and stronger than expected retail sales figures this week - confirming the 'Olympic' boost to its economy - the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained under pressure. \par \par \uc2  "It is out of market favor," said Spiros Papadopoulos, treasur economist with National Australia Bank in London. "Nothing has ignited the market to want to hold Aussie dollars." \par \par \uc2  For the moment, economists remain puzzled about why the Aussie is still at the bottom of the heap. \par \par \uc2  One excuse for not buying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been the fact that Australia has been classifed very much as 'old' economy. However, in recent weeks, it has been the old economy stocks rather than the hi-techers that have been finding favor in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Another problem could be repatriation of Japanese money as Tokyo stocks continue to dive and the Japanese recovery remains in doubt. As has happened in the past, Japanese investors tend to bring their cash home in time of uncertainty. \par \par \uc2  However, it could well be Australian interest rates that prove the problem - and the Aussie's possible rescue. \par \par \uc2  With the cash rate now down at only 6.25%, well below U.S. Fed Funds at 6.50%, this could be why the Aussie remains out of favor. If the RBA finds itself forced to raise rates next week - if only to help protect the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from more losses - this could prove a turning point. \par \par \uc2  Until early this year, the Aussie was famed for moving in lock-step with rates. \par \par \uc2  But, not all are sure this will be the solution either. \par \par \uc2  "In an environemnt of sharply slower world growth, it will take more than a pronounced movement in spreads that we have seen so far to enocurage investors back into Australia," warned Paul Meggyesi, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Deutsche Bank. \par \par \uc2  -By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; 44-20-7842-9493; Nick.Hastings@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1147 4235 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020010804dwb200rpo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
