\par\par \b \uc2  A$, NZ$ mired near record lows vs US dlr in Europe. \b0\par\par\uc2 424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Oktober 2000\par 12:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  LONDON, Oct 9 (Reuters) - The Australian and New Zealand dollars were mired near last week's record lows against the U.S. dollar in European trade on Monday amid a dearth of investor interest to move their funds out of the United States. \par \par \uc2  Although U.S. stocks sold off on concerns of higher interest rates after Friday's stronger-than-expected U.S. September employment report, a flight-to-quality funds shift occurred mainly to U.S. government bonds, keeping the U.S. dollar resilient across the board, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "It's quite clear that international investors are not particularly keen on buying Australian or New Zealand assets for now," said Jane Foley, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital in London. \par \par \uc2  "I think there's a strong chance that the Australian and New Zealand dollars will continue to make new lows against the U.S. dollar." \par \par \uc2  The Aussie dollar was hovering around US$0.5330 , off a quarter cent from early peaks and about half a cent away from last week's record lows below US$0.53. \par \par \uc2  The kiwi dollar was trading around US$0.4025 pence, also giving back a quarter cent from early peaks to dwindle toward last week's life lows below US$0.40. \par \par \uc2  A market holiday in Japan and the United States on Monday kept trading thin, particularly as traders waited to see possible fallout from the failure of Japan's Chiyoda Mutual Life Insurance Co and from the recent slide in U.S. stocks. \par \par \uc2  Chiyoda's filing for court protection was the biggest corporate bankruptcy in Japan, with total debt of 2.94 trillion yen (US$26.95). \par \par \uc2  Lingering wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the Aussie, as well as that by Group of Seven industrial powers to prop up the euro also kept the market jittery, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "If we see another round of coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the euro can make a sustainable turnaround, that would help the Aussie and the kiwi," said a dealer at a Japanese bank in London. \par \par \uc2  "But as long as the euro is stable as it is at the moment, I don't think they're going to do it today." \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar was steady amid a market holiday in Canada, consolidating after a fairly big two-day selloff late last week. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar was last trading at C$1.5030/40 per U.S. dollar , virtually unchanged from Friday's close. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwa904g0s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
