\par\par \b \uc2  Australian Dlr Hits Another New Low; Outlook Still Bleak \b0\par\par\uc2  By Clyde Russell \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 575 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2000\par 03:36\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY -(Dow Jones)- The Australian dollar hit another record low Friday as it continued its inexorable downward march in the face of a weak euro and a strong U.S. dollar, and many traders are reluctant to forecast where the trend will end. \par \par \uc2  A wave of selling drove the Australian dollar down to a low of US$0.5285 as stop-loss orders were triggered around US$0.5300, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Trade was described by dealers as choppy, with several reporting a "mistrade" at US$0.5280 early Friday. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had recovered to around US$0.5300 by 0115 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The previous record low of US$0.5337 was hit Wednesday after the Reserve Bank of Australia's surprise decision to keep rates on hold. The market had been expecting a rise to combat rising inflationary pressures. \par \par \uc2  Ironically, it was the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to raise its rates that prompted the latest bout of Australian dollar weakness as the euro slid after the hike on the view that this could hurt euro-zone growth prospects. \par \par \uc2  "The bias is still towards the downside," said IBJ Australia dealer Scott Lewis, adding that market participants are nervous about calling a bottom level for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Although the Reserve Bank appeared to be on the sidelines, the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  both in \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and by global central banks in support of the weaker euro is preventing market watchers from going too short. \par \par \uc2  Greg McKenna, market strategist at National Australia Bank, said the Australian dollar is targeting US$0.5250, which he said is an "easy get," and US$0.5130 below that level. \par \par \uc2  "If that gives way, we're looking at a change of the big figure," he said. \par \par \uc2  He also pointed out that the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is weakening against the euro as well as other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even slipping to the bottom of its two-to-three-year trading band against the persistently weak South African rand. \par \par \uc2  McKenna said that the Reserve Bank missed an opportunity to tighten Wednesday, after its October board meeting Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "If going forward they seek to tighten and the market perceives the economy to be past the point of absorbing a rate hike without slowing, then the Australian dollar will likely suffer the same fate as the euro," McKenna said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                Bottom May Be In Sight\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Clifford Bennett, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at BNP Paribas, said\par while he continues to respect the downside for the Australian dollar,\par the bottom may be in sight, especially as market commentators are now\par "competing to outdo each other in their degree of bearishness."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "This is a significant warning that the major low point of this cycle\par isn't far away in terms of time. In price, the turn could be immediate,\par or from still lower levels," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bennett said the Australian dollar "remains fundamentally comfortable\par in the US$0.54-0.57 area long term.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "There will be market swings beyond this range in both directions," he\par said, adding there is still a risk of US$0.5000 for the Australian\par dollar short term.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The last few days of a trend can be particularly damaging in terms of\par price. For now, while below US$0.5400, I suggest continued respect for\par the downtrend," Bennett said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Clyde Russell, Dow Jones Newswires, 612 8235-2958\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par clyde.russell@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dwa604t2n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
