\par\par \b \uc2  Australian Mkts Expected Cautious After Euro Invervention \b0\par\par\uc2  By Andrew Trounson \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 807 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2000\par 06:03\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MELBOURNE -(Dow Jones)- Caution will likely be the watch word for Australian financial markets at the start of this week following the surprise \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday by major central banks to support the ailing euro. \par \par \uc2  While the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will silence the euro bears in the short term and thereby help prop up a falling Australian dollar, the failure of both \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold their initial gains means the jury is still out on whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will succeed in restoring monetary stability. \par \par \uc2  What is more, there is a risk the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , thought to be just the first shot in a longer campaign, could eventually destabilize the U.S. dollar and undermine U.S. stocks as a result. \par \par \uc2  "They've opened a Pandora's box here," Greg McKenna, market strategist at National Australia Bank Ltd. (NAB) in Sydney, told Dow Jones Newswires Sunday. \par \par \uc2  "The strength of the U.S. stock market and the strength of U.S. dollar are inextricably tied up," he said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , led by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the U.S. Federal Reserve, the Bank of Japan, the Bank of Canada and a number of Euro-zone banks, pushed the euro briefly above US$0.9000 Friday, but the unit has since fallen back to US$0.8768. \par \par \uc2  Similarly, the Australian dollar initially jumped to US$0.5593 following the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , up from an all-time low of US$0.5363 earlier in the week, but later retreated to US$0.5440. There are reports Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , may take the opportunity to use the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the other banks as cover to itself help support the Australian dollar late Friday. \par \par \uc2  On Saturday, the Group of Seven finance ministers and central banks issued a statement from their meeting in Prague that leaves the door open for further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "In light of recent developments, we will continue to monitor developments closely and to cooperate in exchange markets as appropriate," the ministers and central bankers said. \par \par \uc2  While the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and uncertainty over whether more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is coming, will help support the euro and Australian dollar in the short term, McKenna warned it isn't certain that any euro strength will fully filter through to the Australian dollar. \par \par \uc2  "It isn't obvious from the price action in the Australian dollar that the worst is over," McKenna said. \par \par \uc2  "The Australian dollar has probably made its low for the time being, but any upside impetus towards US$0.5800 or US$0.5900 is probably going to cap it in the immediate term in the absence of a really concerted selloff in the U.S. dollar," McKenna said. \par \par \uc2  Any signs that the Australian dollar has bottomed, however, could translate into support for Australian bond prices relative to U.S. Treasurys. \par \par \uc2  Despite strength on Wall Street Friday, Australian stocks are expected to open cautiously Monday, with the Olympics in Sydney likely to be an ongoing distraction for investors. \par \par \uc2  However, just as with bonds, any expectation that the Australia dollar has bottomed could prompt some bargain buying from offshore, particularly in the resources sector where takeover rumors abound. \par \par \uc2  The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 0.76% Friday, buoyed by the U.S. government's decision to damp oil prices by releasing stock from its Strategic Petroleum Reserve. The technology sensitive Nasdaq composite index, however, fell 0.66%, weighed on by Intel Corp.'s (INTC) warning Thursday of a revenue shortfall. \par \par \uc2  Australia's All Ordinaries Index dropped 1.9% to 3143.5 Friday in anticipation of further weakness on Wall Street, capping a loss for the week of almost 4.2%. \par \par \uc2  Domestic statistics due out this week are unlikely to shed any fresh light on the outlook for Australian rates, with a 0.25% hike in official rates still being tipped for October. However, the Reserve Bank's credit aggregates for August, due out Friday, will be closely watched. \par \par \uc2  In a weekly outlook Friday, Westpac Banking Corp. (WBK) said it expects a further "modest" slowdown in housing credit growth in August following some slowing in July, while personal credit growth is expected to be steady following some slowing also in July. \par \par \uc2  "This result would go a little way further to allaying the RBA's concerns regarding household credit growth, however, it will still be running at a strong pace," Westpac said. \par \par \uc2  In contrast, UBS Warburg Australia Ltd. said Friday in its weekly outlook that it is expecting a "solid" growth in private credit in August following July's slowdown. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Other statistics due out this week include job vacancies data for the\par August quarter on Thursday, while the August trade balance and motor\par vehicle registrations are both due Friday.\par    -By Andrew Trounson, Dow Jones Newswires; 61-3-9614-2664;\par andrew.trounson@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw9o04lkz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
