\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 2-Australian dollar hits all-time low. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Colin Brinsden \par \uc2 482 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 2000\par 07:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SYDNEY, Sept 13 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar sank to a record low 55.25 U.S. cents on Wednesday despite new data showing an upbeat Australian economy had notched up its 13th consecutive quarter of annual growth above four percent. \par \par \uc2  Official data recorded second quarter GDP growth at a respectable 0.7 percent for a year-on-year rate of 4.7 percent, but below bullish expectations. \par \par \uc2  "I think it's still a pretty good result. It wasn't quite the gold medal outcome we were expecting, but it's worthy of at least a silver," said Michael Blythe, chief research economist at Commonwealth Bank of Australia. \par \par \uc2  However, it wasn't enough to satisfy \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets which had looked for 1.4 percent quarterly growth and 5.2 percent annual, expecting such an outcome to force the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hand on monetary policy and help stem the dollar's recent slide. \par \par \uc2  Shortly after the late morning release of the data, the Aussie broke through its previous low of 55.30 U.S cents, set on August 27, 1998 and equalled in London trade last Thursday. \par \par \uc2  It rebounded to around to 55.50 amid talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBA), but sagged again by late afternoon. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the outlook for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains negative as it plumbs new territory, with some calling it as low as 52.50 U.S. cents. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar - along with other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as Sterling, the euro and the New Zealand dollar - has struggled in recent months as investors pour their attention on the strong returns in U.S. asset markets. \par \par \uc2  EXPORTS TO UNDERPIN GROWTH \par \par \uc2  Treasurer Peter Costello remained positive on Australia's economic growth, saying the June quarter meant the economy retained considerable momentum going into 2000/01. \par \par \uc2  Further growth would be driven by exports, which increased by 29 percent in the year to June 2000. "Export growth would be supported by strong world growth, the lower exchange rate and a boost from the Sydney Olympics," Costello said. \par \par \uc2  While the government was obviously watching the exchange rate closely, he tied the recent weakness to the rise of the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Aussie was already on a renewed downward path before the GDP report after consumer sentiment dropped 8.1 percent in September, although this followed a cumulative 20.3 percent increase in the three preceding months. \par \par \uc2  "Current sentiment levels are still consistent with solid consumer spending and do not alter our view that the economy retains significant momentum," said Bill Evans, general manager, economics at Westpac. \par \par \uc2  Evans expected the RBA to raise rates by around 50 basis points over the next few months. \par \par \uc2  The interest rate market posted a modest rally on the GDP data, but still have a quarter point tightening risk priced in by year-end. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9d086ra\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
