\par\par \b \uc2  Australian Dlr Pushes Above US$0.5600 On Weekend Cover \b0\par\par\uc2 305 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2000\par 02:27\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  CANBERRA -(Dow Jones)- Short covering in thin trading has helped the Australian dollar push above US$0.5600 late Friday morning. \par \par \uc2  After skimming overnight its all-time low set in August 1998 of US$0.5530, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has recovered nearly three quarters of a U.S. cent. \par \par \uc2  At 0015 GMT, the Australian dollar was quoted at US$0.5607, up from an overnight low of US$0.5530 but still below US$0.5639 in late Sydney trading Thursday. \par \par \uc2  "The market is a little bit nervous about going into the weekend" with short positions ahead of a meeting of European finance ministers, said Geoff Bowmer, a division director at Macquarie Bank Ltd. \par \par \uc2  He said while most market participants don't expect the informal meeting of finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors to result in any coordinated action to boost the euro, even the remote chance of that happening has the bears pulling back. \par \par \uc2  "Even the bears go into hibernation sometimes," said Greg McKenna, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at National Australia Bank. \par \par \uc2  If European finance ministers do agree to act, it "would provide a circuit breaker for the whole market," including the Australian dollar, Bowmer said. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar's recent woes have been partly blamed on a slump in the euro to record lows. The euro fell to a fresh low overnight of US$0.8630 but has since recovered. Around 0022 GMT, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at US$0.8733. \par \par \uc2  One market participant Thursday described the euro's spectacular fall as a bubble in reverse. \par \par \uc2  Traders say liquidity remains thin but there hasn't been talk of any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  in local trading so far Friday. \par \par \uc2  -Iain McDonald, Dow Jones Newswires; 61-2-6208-0901; iain.mcdonald@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw9804eks\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
