\par\par \b \uc2  PRESS DIGEST-Australian General News - Sept 8. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,017 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 September 2000\par 23:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (Compiled for Reuters by Media Monitors) \par \par \uc2  THE AUSTRALIAN FINANCIAL REVIEW \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar fell almost to an all-time record low yesterday despite reported \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the release of strong employment growth figures for August. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dropped to US55.39 cents in Europe after the Bank of England prompted further buying of the American dollar by announcing it would not raise interest rates. Page 1. \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  There have been renewed calls for a royal commission to be held into Western Australia's finance broking scandal following investigations by The Australian Financial Review which have implicated another senior Liberal Party figure in the crisis. The Liberal Party's Perth division president David Siglin is reportedly overdue in repaying a A$400,000 loan arranged through a firm at the centre of the scandal. Page 1. \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  Hundreds of businesses in Melbourne's central business district are taking dramatic precautionary measures to protect themselves from violent demonstrations that have been foreshadowed in the lead-up to the World Economic Forum next week. Many retailers have boarded up their stores and the Victorian police force is calling in supplementary staff. Page 3. \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  Telecommunications giant Telstra yesterday announced that it planned to challenge the Australian Competition and Consumer Commission's method of determining interconnect pricing. Telstra will challenge the Commission's pricing method before the Australian Competition Tribunal because it did not take into account 'real-world' Australian conditions. Page 3. \par \par \uc2  THE AUSTRALIAN \par \par \uc2  Investigations were continuing last night into an incident in which Australian customs officials detained an Uzbekian trainer at Sydney Airport after discovering clearly labelled vials of the banned sports drug human growth hormones in his luggage. The unnamed wrestling trainer was questioned for several hours and is now facing expulsion from the Sydney 2000 Olympic Games. Page 1. \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  The continued fall of the Australian dollar and the release of strong jobs and housing price figures has increased speculation that the Reserve Bank of Australia will increase official interest rates again. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought Australian dollars for the second day in a row yesterday but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still briefly dropped to its lowest level since August 1998. Page 1. \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  Under the Federal Government's information technology outsourcing policy United States-based law firm Shaw Pittman Potts and Trowbridge has been paid more than A$17 million since 1996. An audit by the Federal Auditor-General found that the Government had paid three Canberra-based Shaw Pittman staff up to A$1.7 million per year each for their consultancy work. Page 3. \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  Convicted rapist Leonard John Fraser was yesterday found guilty in the Brisbane Supreme Court of murdering Rockhampton schoolgirl Keyra Steinhardt and will be sentenced in November. Fraser has previously served 19 years in jail for three rapes and two attempted rapes and had also been committed to stand trial for the murder of 19-year-old Sylvia Benedetti. Page 3. \par \par \uc2  THE SYDNEY MORNING HERALD \par \par \uc2  Foreign Affairs Minister Alexander Downer has told Indonesian Ambassador to Australia Arizal Effendi that militia leader Eurico Guterres is responsible for the death of up to six United Nations workers in West Timor on Tuesday. Downer said he hoped the move to send two battalions of the Indonesian Army into West Timor was a sign that Indonesia was finally acting to control the militias. Page 1. \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  International Olympic Committee medical director Patrick Schamasch yesterday announced that top-ranked athletes competing at the Sydney Olympics would be targeted with the new test for the drug EPO. The announcement came as Customs officials revealed that they had seized several vials of suspected human growth hormone in the luggage of an Uzbekistan coach. Page 1. \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  Claims that members of the jury in the John Newman murder trial were contacted after the completion of the trial have been referred to the Independent Commission Against Corruption following a recently completed Sheriff's Office investigation into the matter. The news comes only days after broadcaster John Laws received a suspended prison sentence for interviewing a juror on-air. Page 1. \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  NRMA Insurance Group yesterday exceeded market expectations by posting a net profit of A$296.4 million but still saw its share price slump below the A$2.75 listing price for the first time. Analysts believe concerns about higher claims costs in the New South Wales motor insurance market and the cost of defraying this with reinsurance are responsible for the share price fall. Page 1. \par \par \uc2  THE AGE \par \par \uc2  The Australian Customs Service yesterday revealed it had seized several vials of what appeared to be human growth hormone from a suitcase carried by a coach with the Uzbekistan Olympic team. The coach was detained at Sydney Airport for questioning yesterday but was reportedly released yesterday and is now in residence at the Olympic village. Page A1. \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  Former boxer Ruben 'Hurricane' Carter arrived in Australia yesterday to join Nelson Mandela at a concert for World Reconciliation Day at Melbourne's Colonial Stadium. Carter spent more than 20 years in prison for a murder conviction that was later overturned and yesterday said people all over the world needed to be healed from the pain and hatred of bigotry. Page A1. \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  Victorian Premier Steve Bracks yesterday released documents that reportedly suggest that former Premier Jeff Kennett was considering a trial of supervised injecting facilities as a viable option. Bracks said the documents quite clearly show that the former Victorian Government was willing to negotiate with the Wesley Central Mission for the trial of an injecting facility. Page A3. \par \par \uc2  - \par \par \uc2  Researchers at the Jean Hailes Foundation in Victoria will trial testosterone skin patches that can reportedly restore libido in women who have had their ovaries removed. The Australian trials follow recent testing in the United States which found that women who had undergone a hysterectomy reported a increased sex drive when treated with the patches. Page A1. - \par \par \uc2  Looking for more information from local sources? Reuters Business Briefing has 146 Australian sources including The Australian, Sydney Morning Herald, and The Age. For details of the product, please call your local help desk . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9708x5o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
