\par\par \b \uc2  WRAP: NZ Fin Min Tries To Counter Negative Perceptions \b0\par\par\uc2  By Clyde Russell \par \uc2 604 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juli 2000\par 07:47\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY -(Dow Jones)- New Zealand Finance Minister Michael Cullen tried to counter negative perceptions of his government's economic policies Wednesday in his first address to Australian business leaders and economists. \par \par \uc2  He also used the opportunity to say the New Zealand dollar is undervalued and gave the mildest of hints that interest rates should remain on hold for a while. \par \par \uc2  Cullen told the Trans-Tasman Business Circle that New Zealand has simply moved to the center under the Labour-Alliance coalition. \par \par \uc2  "Far from lurching to the left, we have made a modest move to the center," Cullen said, adding that \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in his view, remains more \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist\b0 \highlight\uc2  and less orthodox than New Zealand. \par \par \uc2  Cullen said his government aims for "greater consistency between Australian and New Zealand law," although he said he has little interest in a unified \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "He tried to deflect a lot of concerns of international investors. He is battling the perceptions of his government," said Macquarie Bank Fixed-Income Analyst David Bassanese. \par \par \uc2  Bassanese said international investors aren't aware of the "intricacies" of the policy changes by the New Zealand government, elected last year, and this had caused an erosion of business and consumer confidence. \par \par \uc2  Cullen said he has been "disturbed" by comments filtering back to New Zealand that the government has changed its attitude toward foreign investment, and much of his speech was spent assuring that his government is seeking to create a better environment for overseas investors. \par \par \uc2  "There has been no anti-investment policy switch, and if anything the new government is pending more, not less, to attract foreign investment," Cullen said. \par \par \uc2  He tried to allay fears over the review of how the Reserve Bank of New Zealand conducts monetary policy, saying the review aimed to see what tools the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs to be more effective. \par \par \uc2  "The independence of the bank and its primary focus on price stability aren't open for review. We just want to improve \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  if that is possible," Cullen said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par            Hints Rates Shouldn't Rise Further\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In questions later with journalists, Cullen hinted that the Reserve\par Bank should be more cautious in raising interest rates over the next few\par months as economic growth in New Zealand is being led by exports, which\par is less inflationary.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We'll wait and see, it depends on (Reserve Bank of New Zealand\par Governor Don) Brash's assumptions on growth and whether that is export\par led," Cullen said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par New Zealand's official cash rate is 6.5%, and the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's last\par statement was interpreted as pointing toward further rate rises in the\par second half of the year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Macquarie's Bassanese said if rates are put on hold, it will be good\par news for the New Zealand economy.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The Reserve Bank (of New Zealand) has been too aggressive in raising\par rates," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Despite the higher interest rates, the New Zealand dollar has suffered\par as investors took the view the tight monetary policy will harm growth.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The New Zealand \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has dropped 11% against the U.S. dollar since\par the beginning of the year to trade at US$0.4597 Wednesday. Its\par performance has been worse than its trans-Tasman neighbor, the\par Australian dollar, which has fallen about 9.3% this year to trade\par currently around US$0.5880.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Both \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are undervalued, Cullen said. "Clearly the Australian\par dollar and the New Zealand dollar are undervalued. That's a reflection\par of market sentiment, and eventually market sentiment will change," he\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Clyde Russell, Dow Jones Newswires, 612 8235-2958\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par clyde.russell@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw7c03e62\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
