\par\par \b \uc2  Australian Dlr Steady Early; Bonds Up; Awaiting RBA \b0\par\par\uc2 452 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2000\par 00:34\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY -(Dow Jones)- The Australian dollar is steady early Monday, maintaining its gains from Friday, but dealers are awaiting testimony from Reserve Bank of Australian Governor Ian Macfarlane later in the session for market direction. \par \par \uc2  Australian bond futures are slightly higher, especially at the short end, matching a rise in U.S. Treasury values in New York trading Friday. \par \par \uc2  At 2220 GMT, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at US$0.5725, little changed from US$0.5728 late Friday in Sydney. The local unit was also at Y61.30, slightly up from Y62.15. \par \par \uc2  Paul McNee, chief dealer at Australia & New Zealand Banking Group, expects the local unit to trade between US$0.5650 and US$0.5750 Monday, with any rise above US$0.5750 likely to trigger stop-loss buying. \par \par \uc2  Any indication by Macfarlane that the Reserve Bank will continue to match U.S. interest rate rises will cause the Australian dollar to rally, McNee said. But he thinks the bias is still on the downside given the overwhelming perception that Australia won't match U.S. rate rises. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's semiannual testimony is scheduled to begin at 10 a.m. local time Monday, or 0000 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar rose Friday on rumors of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some dealers suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the options market through National \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  Bank to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  While a dealer at National Australia Bank confirmed the bank was in the options market, he wouldn't say who the bank was acting for. \par \par \uc2  Australian bonds are higher early Monday given a rise in Treasury values in New York trading Friday. \par \par \uc2  The bond market is also awaiting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  testimony at 0000 GMT. Any sign that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to raise interest rates will weigh on bond prices, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Bond traders are also awaiting the possible announcement of a government bond tender at 0200 GMT for the sale of bonds Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Bond traders expect the government to sell between A$600 million to A$800 million of a new 2013 benchmark bond. The tender would go well given a recent steepening in the yield curve, which would represent good yield pick up, traders said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    9.50% Aug. bond '03:   6.47% vs.  6.54%\par    7.50% Sep. bond '09:   6.56% vs.  6.61%\par    SFE Jun 3Y Futures:   93.54  up   0.08\par    SFE Jun 10Y Futures:  93.435 up   0.040\par    3-10 Yr Yield Spread   0.09  vs.  0.07\par    10-Yr Spread To U.S.:  0.07  vs.  0.07\par   -By Nicki Bourlioufas, 61-2-8235-2959, nicki.bourlioufas@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1054 22765 4109 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw5l02imi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
