\par\par \b \uc2  Australian Dollar Lower Early, Bonds Expected Sharply  Down \b0\par\par\uc2 597 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Mai 2000\par 00:12\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY -(Dow Jones)- The Australian dollar is lower early Thursday but with the market off its low of US$0.5657, reached in New York dealings. \par \par \uc2  Government bonds are expected to open sharply lower, depressed by the ever-weakening Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the decline in Treasurys prices Wednesday on the back of expectations for further U.S. rate increases in coming months. \par \par \uc2  At 2210 GMT, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at US$0.5690, down from US$0.5710 late Wednesday. The local unit was also at Y62.14, down from Y62.33. \par \par \uc2  The market touched US$0.5657 for the first time since early September 1998. But the market didn't have enough drive to push it through that level in New York dealings although most say it will likely be tested in Australian trading. \par \par \uc2  Some dealers said the market has got itself quite short and that there may be some squaring up of positions ahead of any push lower. That move may see the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  push above US$0.5700 but it's progress will likely be stalled as selling interest kicks in at US$0.5720, say dealers. \par \par \uc2  On the downside, while US$0.5657 is a previous low, dealers say that US$0.5650 is the key psychological focus of support for the market. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people had expressed an interest in buying the Aussie below 56 (U.S. cents) and I think this is the sort of level where people are waiting to see if anyone comes up to the plate," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Westpac Banking Corp. \par \par \uc2  "There's no interest around from anyone to buy the Aussie dollar at the moment so I think people are grasping at any level," said the dealer. \par \par \uc2  Some market players are becoming increasingly convinced that the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at these levels but most dealers point out the market's fall hasn't been disorderly - a key factor in bringing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  into the market. \par \par \uc2  They also say the Reserve Bank would be unwilling to try to fight what is largely a story of U.S. dollar strength against a range of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Government bonds are lower with dealers suggesting all eyes are on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide. The U.S. Treasurys market also added its own dose of negative sentiment, having closed lower Wednesday on renewed interest rate fears. \par \par \uc2  The widening interest differential to the U.S., which now stands at 50 basis points at the short end of the yield curve, will increasingly justify a negative yield spread to Treasurys further out along the curve, say dealers. \par \par \uc2  "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's really what we're watching at the moment," said a bond dealer here. "There seemed to be some support at 57 (U.S. cents), but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps on pushing through that level so it's not looking good." \par \par \uc2  The yield curve has steepened this session, swinging from a flattening Wednesday as the short end underperformed longer maturities after the U.S. 50 basis point rate hike added pressure for further rate cuts in Australia. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    9.50% Aug. bond '03:   6.48% vs.  6.36%\par    7.50% Sep. bond '09:   6.52% vs.  6.43%\par    SFE Jun 3Y Futures:   93.48  dn   0.04\par    SFE Jun 10Y Futures:  93.480 dn   0.070\par    3-10 Yr Yield Spread   0.04  vs. -0.03\par    10-Yr Spread To U.S.:  0.04  vs.  0.03\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Adam Bradbery; 61-2-8235-2955;\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par adam.bradbery@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1054 22765 4109 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw5h02ha1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
