\par\par \b \uc2  Fed Rate Hike Puts Pressure On Other Central Banks \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jennifer Barrett \par \uc2 989 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2000\par 21:35\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK Dow Jones)--Some major central banks may be having second thoughts about matching the Federal Reserve's aggressive half-point rate hike after rate increases Wednesday in New Zealand and Canada not only failed to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from falling against the dollar,  but accelerated the slide. \par \par \uc2  After the Reserve Bank of New Zealand raised its rates, the New Zealand dollar plunged more than one U.S. cent to a 14-year low of $0.4592. despite a 50-basis-point  hike by the Bank of Canada, the U.S. dollar gained over a  cent Wednesday against the Canadian dollar. Late Wednesday, the dollar was at C$1.4977, compared with C$1.4865 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The increase in U.S. interest rates and rally in stock  prices Tuesday proved a potent combination for the dollar's counterparts, with the dollar strengthening against nearly every widely-traded \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Fed's decision. \par \par \uc2  The New Zealand Reserve Bank, Bank of Canada, Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and Philippines \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  responded with matching half-point hikes Wednesday morning. Meanwhile, the Bank of Mexico raised its money market shortfall - an action that tends to boost domestic interest rates. \par \par \uc2  The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to raise its rates by 25 basis points either when it meets next week or  at the following meeting in early June. The Reserve Bank of Australia is also contemplating an increase in its lending rates when it meets next month. \par \par \uc2  But some question now whether such action will help or  hinder the Australian and euro-zone \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which have each lost between 11%-13% of their value against the dollar since the start of the year. \par \par \uc2  Higher interest rates are typically viewed as being beneficial to the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the increased yields on locally-denominated assets attracts foreign investors. But the New Zealand, Canadian and Mexican rate hikes had the opposite effect on their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  That's largely because the U.S. economy is outpacing theirs, and investors fear the matching hikes may slow the  economies down even further in those countries. \par \par \uc2  "The relationship between interest rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not linear as we're often led to believe," said Marc Chandler, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Pittsburgh-based Mellon Financial Corp. "Clearly, this shows the relationship between \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and interest rates is much more complicated." \par \par \uc2  Andy Busch, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Bank of Montreal in Chicago, said the New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision left him shaking his head in disbelief, particularly in light of recent data showing the New Zealand economy is already clearly slowing down. \par \par \uc2  "I'm not quite sure what they were trying to accomplish, but it certainly did not help the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Busch. \par \par \uc2  In fact, dealers blame the New Zealand dollar's fall for dragging down other dollar-block \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in neighboring Australia and in Canada. \par \par \uc2  After the Reserve Bank of New Zealand raised its rates, the New Zealand dollar plunged more than one U.S. cent to a 14-year low of $0.4592. The Australian dollar followed suit, plunging to $0.5660 at about 11:20 a.m. EDT  - the first time it has fallen below 57 cents in 21 months. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday, the New Zealand dollar was at $0.4612.  The Australian dollar was at $0.5682. \par \par \uc2  The Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rapid decline following Tuesday's FOMC decision has raised speculation that the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prop up its local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or raise rates when it next meets to narrow interest rate differentials with the U.S. In recent public statements, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has expressed concern about the inflationary impact of a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In the Philippines, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its overnight borrowing and lending rates by 50 basis points to 9.50% and 11.75%. \par \par \uc2  The move managed to slow but not stop the dollar's ascent on the Philippine peso. Late Wednesday, the dollar was trading at PHP41.65, down slightly from earlier in the  day, when the dollar hit PHP41.690, its highest level in 19 months \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the peso's weakness is also a result of domestic problems, including the country's modest growth prospects, slow domestic demand recovery, political uncertainties and the government's ongoing battle against separatist rebels in the south. \par \par \uc2  The Philippines was not the only Asian country to react to the Fed rate hike. \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong's \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its base lending  rate a half-point to 8.0% Wednesday. Its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is pegged against the dollar, though, and remained nearly flat in trade with the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .. \par \par \uc2  Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also rumored to be checking with banks on That baht rates against the dollar despite the national holiday, fueling speculation it may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the struggling local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it fell to 39.50 to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday, the dollar was at THB39.435, up from 39.135 early in the European session, and down just slightly from its intraday high of THB39.450. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Mexico also raised its daily money market shortfall by MXN20 million to MXN200 million Tuesday, a move that generally boosts domestic interest rates. While it momentarily strengthened the peso Tuesday, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell back on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Late Wednesday, the dollar was buying 9.5450 pesos, up  from MXN9.504 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "People continue to look at the dollar and the U.S. economy relative\par to elsewhere in the world and see a machine that's just chugging along\par and a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that continues to be deserving of a higher valuation,"\par said Ben  Strauss, assistant vice president of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Bank\par Julius Baer in New York. "The dollar is king."\par    -By Jennifer Barrett, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-4384\par email:jennifer.barrett@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1142 4218 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dj00000020010805dw5h021ty\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
