\par\par \b \uc2  Australian Dollar Up Early But Bond Prices Drop Overnight \b0\par\par\uc2 682 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2000\par 21:55\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SYDNEY -(Dow Jones)- The Australian dollar is starting Tuesday's local session slightly higher, after trading as low at US$0.6107 in overnight trade. \par \par \uc2  Australian government bond prices will likely open lower after values of bond futures contracts slipped in tandem with U.S. Treasurys overnight. \par \par \uc2  At 2245 GMT, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quoted at US$0.6142, up a little from US$0.6133 late Monday. The local unit was also at Y67.21, up from Y66.77 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  Bond prices are set to open lower after U.S. Treasurys registered sharp losses Monday in response to a recovery in equity prices. \par \par \uc2  In overnight computerized trading, March three-year bond futures last traded at 93.50, down from 93.55 locally Monday, on volume of 7,200 contracts, while the March 10-year bond contract last changed hands at 93.300 on rather low volume of 2,600. That was down from Monday's settlement of 93.365. \par \par \uc2  Local fund managers reverted to shortening portfolio duration last week, according to Stone & McCarthy Research Associates' weekly survey of fund managers. The shortening trend has been in place since the Reserve Bank of Australia started to hike rates in November, Stone & McCarthy said. \par \par \uc2  Average market duration is now 3.39 years, compared with 3.49 in the previous week, and that is close to the lowest recorded since Stone & McCarthy's survey began in mid-1996. \par \par \uc2  Most of the shortening was done from already short or neutral positions. It took the proportion of fund managers short to 73%, up from 61%. \par \par \uc2  "This could be the start of a new bear phase for the market with the curve steepening view up sharply, and the number of funds looking to shorten never higher in the past 18 months," said Stone & McCarthy. \par \par \uc2  The only data due Tuesday are Reserve Bank of Australia statistics on financial aggregates. The January data will be released at 0030 GMT. \par \par \uc2  As has been the case lately, there was more action in the Australian dollar overnight than in the local session. It traded between US$0.6105 and US$0.6171 in offshore trading Monday. \par \par \uc2  Nick Volanakis, manager of marketing of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Colonial State Bank, said most of the damage was done in local trading Monday after the release of weaker-than-expected fourth quarter inventories data and an unexpected widening of the international trade deficit in January. However, the selling continued in London with a U.S. bank seen selling the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down to its low of US$0.6105, he said. \par \par \uc2  Apart from the local data, the Australian dollar has also been battered by the strength of the yen. Along with the euro, the Australian dollar has also been sold for yen over the last 24 hours. \par \par \uc2  Volanakis said it was encouraging that the Australian dollar didn't fall below US$0.6100 overnight, and he expects it to range between US$0.6105 and US$0.6150 in local trading Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment is still pretty negative, so the local unit will have trouble taking out minor resistance at US$0.6145/50, he added. Beyond that, the next technical levels are US$0.6180 and Friday's overnight high of US$0.6220. Volanakis put support at US$0.6100/05, but a break of that could see some "blood-letting," with US$0.6080 and US$0.6050/60 as the next targets. \par \par \uc2  There is no shortage of things for dealers to keep their eye on this week. January data on retail sales and building approvals will be released Wednesday, followed by quarterly wages data Thursday and the fourth quarter current account deficit on Friday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Volanakis said dealers aren't ruling out the possibility of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central\par bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , noting Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Australia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Assistant Governor\par Ric Battellino was "jawboning" the dollar Friday. At a conference,\par Battellino said the Australian dollar is undervalued in fundamental\par terms.\par -By Marion Williams; Dow Jones Newswires; 612-8235-2956;\par  marion.williams@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1054 22765 4109 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw2s013dg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
