\par\par \b \uc2  Australian, NZ dollar slide to multi-month troughs. \b0\par\par\uc2 455 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2000\par 21:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Jan 28 (Reuters) - The Australian and New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbled to multi-month lows against the U.S. dollar on Friday, hit by broad dollar strength and technical selling in illiquid trading conditions. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also said the Australian dollar had come under pressure amid market talk that a large hedge fund had been forced to liquidate its Australian dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  "Nobody really knew what was pushing it. It was very illiquid and the funds were very active," said one \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader. "Stop after stop got set off. People were just dumping positions." \par \par \uc2  Near midday, the Australian dollar extended its overnight losses and slumped as low as $0.6235 against the U.S. dollar, a level not seen since last April. \par \par \uc2  It then quickly pared its losses to orbit $0.6300, lifted amid rumours that the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  (RBA) had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stem the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp slide. \par \par \uc2  A representative at the RBA in New York was unable to confirm or deny this. \par \par \uc2  But the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hung some 3.5 percent lower against the U.S. dollar in late U.S. trading, and dealers were sceptical that its sharp bounce reflected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action. \par \par \uc2  "The price action was such that would suggest that, but it could be hedge fund activity again," said Pete Stahl, trader at Australian and New Zealand Banking Group in New York. \par \par \uc2  The New Zealand dollar, nicknamed the kiwi, came under similar pressure, tumbling to $0.4849, its lowest point in more than 1-1/2 years. The kiwi also pared its losses to stand near $0.49 at 3:00 p.m. (2000 GMT). \par \par \uc2  Traders were somewhat taken aback by the Aussie and kiwi's sharp declines, and they struggled to find something solid on which to pin the moves. \par \par \uc2  "You wouldn't have expected the to move as much as they did. There were obviously some very large sellers, and maybe it's also just thin here in the U.S." said Mark Thome, vice president at Fortis. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's strength against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also helped push the Aussie and kiwi lower, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The greenback rallied to lifetime highs against Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the U.S. session, racing as high as 97.36 cents per euro, and shot to 3-1/2 month highs against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  just above 107 yen. It also bolted to a fresh 10-year high against the Swiss franc of 1.6542 francs. \par \par \uc2  "Anything that was not dollar was getting crushed," a trader said. \par \par \uc2  ((Elizabeth Lazarowitz, U.S. Financial Markets Desk, 212-859-1607)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 2000. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw1s05035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
