\par\par \b \uc2  Asian markets tumble as investors cut and run. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Philippe Naughton \par \uc2 520 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 1998\par 04:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SYDNEY, Aug 28 (Reuters) - Asia-Pacific share markets tumbled early on Friday after a major sell-off on Wall Street appeared to confirm fears of a global economic downturn and investors scrambled for a way out. \par \par \uc2  Japan's benchmark Nikkei average fell 3.55 percent in early trade to 13,901.50 points - its lowest level since Japan's economic bubble burst in 1990. \par \par \uc2  As the \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar again flirted with historic lows despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight, Australian stocks also dived at the start of trade. \par \par \uc2  The benchmark All Ordinaries index fell 94.8 points or 3.7 percent to 2,465.8. New Zealand's NZSE-40 Capital Index plunged more than four percent in the first hour of trading. \par \par \uc2  And economists said there was no end in sight to the carnage as nervous investors cut their losses and ran. \par \par \uc2  "Investors in emerging markets, those who have not already pulled their money out of Asia, will simply pull the plug and take these markets to historic lows," said Grant Fitzner, an economist at HSBC Markets in Sydney. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of people are liquidating their positions now, taking their losses and exiting these markets." \par \par \uc2  The Asian bailout was sparked by a torrid trading session on Wall Street, where the Dow dropped 357 points, or 4.12 percent, to 8166 points - its lowest level since February. \par \par \uc2  Markets were also in turmoil in Canada, which raised official interest rates to protect its dollar, and in Latin America. Toronto stocks spun down six percent, Argentina's main index lost 10.63 percent and Brazil's Bovespa index tumbled 9.95 percent. \par \par \uc2  "I think we are having a frantic exit from equity markets across the world. People are concerned and they are raising cash," Michael Metz, managing director at CIBC Oppenheimer, told Reuters in New York. \par \par \uc2  The global market turmoil has been largely blamed on a financial meltdown in Russia, where the rouble has gone into virtual freefall. \par \par \uc2  Fitzner said Russia's economy accounted for only one percent of global gross domestic product (GDP) but the country had a symbolic importance for emerging market investors. \par \par \uc2  The main victims of the Russian crisis have been commodity-linked \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Australian and Canadian dollars - and agriculture-dependent Latin American markets. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar swept to new record lows, falling to as low as US$0.5530 in New York trade, despite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The New Zealand dollar hit a fresh 12-year low of US$0.4790 overnight. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar shed about one yen to trade at around 140.50 yen after Wall Street's weakness. \par \par \uc2  For many \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders, the commodity link has been just a pretext for speculators trying to find a safe one-way bet. \par \par \uc2  "These guys lost large in Russia and now they're trying to make it up dumping defenceless \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Australian dollar," said one Sydney dealer. "The commodity rationale is just a smokescreen." \par \par \uc2  ((Sydney Newsroom 61-2 9373 1800, sydney.newsroomzreuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021216du8s01pyg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
