\par\par \b \uc2  Australian Dlr/Late -2: Focus On Offshore Mkts Continues \b0\par\par\uc2 362 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 1998\par 09:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Moving into the overnight session, Susi Hawkins, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign\par exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst at Thomson Global Markets, said that while\par short-covering benefited the Australian dollar Friday, problems in\par offshore markets, especially Russia, remain. Consequently, Australian\par dollar risk will remain weighted to the downsdie as turmoil in offshore\par markets continues, and gold and commodity prices remain under pressure.\par    From a technical perspective, Hawkins said that while the Australian\par dollar remains below US$0.5570, there is potential for a retest of the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's post-float low, a break of which should bring into focus\par the next support level at US$0.5475. She added, however, that there is\par potential for the Australian dollar to extend its downtrend to\par US$0.5200-US$0.5300. On the topside, a break above US$0.5570 is seen\par targeting US$0.5630, Hawkins said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  "The Australian dollar is oversold on economic and commodity market fundamentals, unless you believe in the prospect of deep global recession," commented Craig James, chief economist at Colonial State Bank. \par \par \uc2  James said "a circuit-breaker is required to restore stability. The most appropriate form of action is for the U.S. Federal Reserve to ease monetary policy. But first the U.S. needs to be convinced that there is a crisis." \par \par \uc2  Commonwealth Bank of Australia economists Michael Blythe and David Moore, in a weekly report Friday, agreed that the Australian dollar was oversold on the basis of fundamentals, "but the fundamentals are largely irrelevant at present." \par \par \uc2  Commonwealth Bank economists said "the uncertain international economic outlook, pressures on commodity prices and the unsettled state of international financial markets, means some further depreciation of the Australian dollar cannot be ruled out." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par There were unconfirmed reports that the U.S. Federal Reserve\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on behalf of the Reserve Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Australia\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during the\par Australian dollar's slump to overnight lows, according to dealers. While\par there was no talk of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market Friday, traders said\par market participants remain on watch for further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par    -By Sandra Muldoon; 612-9290-8871; smuldoon@ap.org\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires 28-08-98\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0740GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du8s07bcx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
