\par\par \b \uc2  Australian dollar inches ahead as yen recovers. \b0\par\par\uc2 480 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 August 1998\par 08:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SYDNEY, Aug 5 (Reuters) - The Australian dollar ended barely changed on Wednesday after edging up on the back of a firmer yen, but the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains were seen as largely corrective after having lost five figures in just over a week. \par \par \uc2  The medium-term outlook for the yen remains dim until financial markets receive a clearer picture of the new Japanese government's plans to revive the recession-hit economy, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  By 4.35 p.m. (0635 GMT), the Aussie dollar was at $0.6059/64, unchanged from its level in Wednesday's opening trade and $0.6063/68 here late on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The yen won a reprieve overnight as the U.S. dollar tumbled on the back of a 3.4 percent slide in U.S. stock prices, and fresh supportive comments by Japanese officials on Wednesday kept the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  under presssure. \par \par \uc2  The yen rose to a high of 143.38 against the U.S. dollar after a report that \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official Haruhiko Kuroda said the yen's excessive weakness had yet to be corrected. \par \par \uc2  That followed clarification by Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa on Tuesday of comments he had made that appeared to lessen the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen. Miyazawa said he never intended to rule out \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the yen would be helped over coming days by concerns over possible Bank of Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to coincinde with Prime Minister Keizo Obuchi's policy speech on Friday. \par \par \uc2  But any buying of the yen is expected to be confined to Japanese authorities, since the U.S. is seen as unlikely to join in when its stock market is vulnerable and risk triggering a flow of funds out of the U.S. \par \par \uc2  By 0635 GMT, the yen was trading at 143.85/90, about a yen firmer than its early Tokyo level of 144.80/90. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the Aussie closely tracked the yen's moves through the session, retreating from its highs as the yen fell back from its best levels. \par \par \uc2  After trading in a tight range through the morning, the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  made several attempts at breaching resistance at $0.6085 in the afternoon before the effort proved too great. \par \par \uc2  Over the medium term, the trend for the Aussie is still seen lower in line with further yen weakness, despite the brief reprieve on the past two days' flurry of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, local markets may be briefly distracted by \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's July employment report, though the main impetus for trading remains offshore events. \par \par \uc2  Employment is seen rising by 10,000 jobs in July after a 20,200 gain in June, while the unemployment rate is expected to remain at 8.2 percent. \par \par \uc2  ((Victoria Thieberger, Sydney newsroom 61-2 9373-1800, sydney.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020021108du8501qcd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
