\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-World Bank says G7 must do more for Asia. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ruth Pitchford \par \uc2 933 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 1998\par 08:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SYDNEY, June 18 (Reuters) - A senior World Bank official on Thursday urged leading industrialised countries to do more to help Asia's smaller economies, saying such aid would also assist Japan's recovery. \par \par \uc2  Jean-Michel Severino, the bank's vice-president for East Asia and the Pacific region, said the Group of Seven nations should commit more money to fight the Asian economic crisis. \par \par \uc2  "I think the key is that East Asia matters for Japan and Japan matters for the G7," Severino told Reuters Television. \par \par \uc2  Severino made headlines this week with a stark warning that the Asia crisis could turn into a deep and lengthy depression with global repercussions. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, Severino welcomed joint U.S.-Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up the yen that has also buoyed regional equity markets and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But he told a business audience that despite sharp falls in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values over the past year, the crisis was so widespread that countries in the region could not find export markets to fund their way out of trouble. \par \par \uc2  "Each country is exporting its recession to its neighbours," he said. \par \par \uc2  He argued that it was up to the G7 nations to offer soft loans to help governments run budget deficits to boost demand in the region. \par \par \uc2  "This money has to come from the G7 and from the developed countries in the region... Otherwise one should just accept low growth rates and a depression," Severino said. \par \par \uc2  Severino praised China for its commitment to avoid a devaluation of the yuan and applauded U.S. and Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday as "a very positive step forward". \par \par \uc2  But he told Reuters help for the region's weaker economies could also play a vital role in pulling Japan out of recession because East Asia was an important market for Japanese exports and Japanese banks were major creditors to the region. \par \par \uc2  G7 deputy finance ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials, along with representatives from the Asian Development Bank, World Bank and International Monetary Fund, are due to meet in Tokyo on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  Severino said that if the G7 did not help, government spending aimed at reviving the regional economy would simply force up local market interest rates in a vicious circle of recession that was causing great social pain. \par \par \uc2  "The ADB and the World Bank have more or less reached their head limits (on funding)," Severino said, adding it was critical that funds be found for social spending in the region. \par \par \uc2  He did not specify which countries should benefit from soft G7 loans to fund budget deficits. \par \par \uc2  Asked if South Korea was a suitable recipient, he told Reuters, "I think the case is less clear in the case of Korea than maybe for less well-off countries in the region." \par \par \uc2  However, he added that South Korea could not go on paying relatively high rates for loans for too long. "One has to find more reasonable ways of funding, definitely, this country." \par \par \uc2  He praised the South Korean authorities for the speed and scope of their efforts to address the country's problems but added: "They're paying the price through very high and fast rising unemployment." \par \par \uc2  The IMF has led bailout packages to Thailand, Indonesia and South Korea totalling more than US$100 billion since the Asian economic crisis began with sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  declines in mid-1997. \par \par \uc2  Severino told his Australian business audience that there were important differences between the Asian crisis and the Latin American debt crisis of the 1980s, which was stoked by runaway government spending. \par \par \uc2  Asian governments had been relatively blameles in that respect, he said, yet "so far the region has been funded by the world's financial community to a very limited extent, nothing to be compared with the Latin American crisis." \par \par \uc2  ((Sydney newsroom 61-2 9373-1800 sydney.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  Describing the impact of the Asian crisis on the Japanese banking sector as a "very important" factor, he told Reuters: "There is a close link between the recovery in east Asia and the recovery in Japan." \par \par \uc2  Speaking earlier at the luncheon meeting organised by the Committee for the Economic Development of \b \highlight22\uc2 Australia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and HSBC Markets, Severino praised U.S. and Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight to support the yen. \par \par \uc2  However, he declined to offer any more specific views on how the G7 should tackle the problem of Japan's economic weakness. \par \par \uc2  "I will be very modest on this issue. The World Bank is not monitoring, is not involved in Japan and we unfortunately do not have a precise remedy to propose for Japan," he told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  Asked if South Korea was a suitable recipient for soft international loans to help with deficit financing, he said, "I think the case is less clear in the case of Korea than maybe for less well-off countries in the region." \par \par \uc2  However, he added, "This being said we should be reasonable and Korea couldn't afford paying high spreads, of 500 points over LIBOR etc, for a long period of time - one has to find more reasonable ways of funding, definitely, this country." \par \par \uc2  He praised the South Korean authorities for the speed and scope of their efforts to address the country's problems but added, "They're paying the price through very high and fast rising unemployment." \par \par \uc2  The IMF has led bailout packages to Thailand, Indonesia and South Korea totalling more than US$100 billion since the Asian economic crisis began with sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  declines in mid-1997. \par \par \uc2  ((Sydney newsroom 61-2-9373-1800, \par \par \uc2  sydney.newsroom@reuters.com)). \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du6i02rsy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
